Debug.Assert(OLD(s.Count) + x = s.Count);
</code> 

The above assert statement is derived from the comment "@@@ Size of stack increases by x in the function" in the given C# code. It states that before the function call, the Count of stack 's' was OLD(s.Count), and after the function call, it should equal s.Count.

The assert code works as follows: 

```
|--------|-------------------|-------------------|--------|
| Pre-condition | Function call | Post-condition | Expected result |
|--------|-------------------|-------------------|--------|
| s.Count = n before a call Insert(s,x) | Insert(s,x) increments s.Count by x | s.Count = n+x after the call | s.Count increases by x |
|--------|-------------------|-------------------|--------|

```
```

```
In the given C# code, the method Insert() adds the items into the stack. The capacity of the stack increases by the number of items added. The assert statement checks the pre-condition that the count of the stack was n before the function call, then checks the post-condition that after the function call the count increases by x as expected.

It means the assertion ensures that the capacity of the stack is increased as expected when an item is added to the stack. If this fails, it is a sign that the capacity increment is not as expected.

The C# code is designed to increase the capacity of the stack and then add the items. Therefore, if the size of the stack increases as expected, the assertion is correct and does not need to be modified.--------------------